msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "A bill let a merchant settle an account in a city he had never visited: the coin stayed at home and a scrap of paper crossed the road. Such letters of credit circulated in Song China and in the markets of the Islamic world long before European fairs entered them in their ledgers. An outpost beyond the Region was costly chiefly because it lay far away, and the distance could now be paid on paper instead of in coin."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "A bloomery never truly melted its iron: it coaxed a spongy lump from the ore one batch at a time and halted to pull it from the hearth. Raise the shaft, feed it limestone to run the stony gangue off as liquid slag and drive the bellows without pause, and the metal pours from the tap-hole like bright water — brittle under the hammer, yet matchless in the mould and ready to be fined in bulk into bar iron and steel. Cast in China since antiquity and towering beside the millstreams of medieval Europe, the blast furnace turned iron from a smith's batch into an industry."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "A bundle of reeds floats only until it swells with water; a planked hull keeps the water outside. Sawn boards joined edge to edge over curved ribs, stayed with twisted cordage and driven by a square of heavy canvas let boatmen leave the shallows behind, work deeper fishing grounds in rough weather and carry timber and grain along every river and coast."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "A drop of glass bends a ray as surely as a lever lifts a stone. Grinders working crystal and the clear bath of a glass furnace learned to curve a surface until distant letters swam into focus; two such curves set in a frame gave a scholar back the years his eyes had lost. The instrument was small, but the boundary it moved — between what could be read and what could only be guessed — was enormous."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "A field sown with the same grain year after year gives less with every harvest until the earth lies spent. Alternating winter and spring cereals with pulses, fodder and rested fallow — practiced across Chinese bean-and-millet fields, the irrigated gardens of the Islamic world and the three-field manors of medieval Europe — returned strength to the soil and raised more from the same land."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "A firm bed of rubble carries the weight; fitted stones hold the surface together. When the rains come, loaded carts can still reach the city."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "A hotter fire changes the clay itself: the body vitrifies and mineral glazes melt into a skin that holds water. Potters reached such heat in East Asia and in the workshops of the Islamic world long before it became common elsewhere, reading the flame and mixing fluxes from ash and metal oxides. With a kiln that holds the heat through a whole firing, a workshop turns a household craft into a daily trade."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "A map shows where one road meets the next. Surveyors set the width, crews clear the ditches, and each stretch has someone responsible for its upkeep."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "A page cut in wood or cast in metal gives as many copies as the press can pull, and no copy argues with another. The woodblock came first in China, movable type followed there and in Korea, where the letters were cast in bronze; Gutenberg set the same idea in lead and gave it the screw press and paper. A scribe copies a book in a year, a workshop prints it in a season."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "A plow yoked to oxen made it possible to work heavy soils and raised yields."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "A protoscience of attempts to turn base metals into gold."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "A shaft is only as deep as the rope that serves it. A drum on a windlass, a rope over a pulley, a capstan walked round by men or oxen — and in the Chinese mines a treadwheel the size of a house, turning all day like the water wheel it resembles. What the hoist brings up is what the mine is worth: the ore that cannot be lifted stays in the rock."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "A threaded spindle turns many easy turns of a long handle into one slow, enormous squeeze. The press no longer waits on the weight of a beam, and a second batch can be worked beside the first."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "A wheel that rolls is not yet a machine; the teeth are. They turn one turn of the axle into a turn of the millstone, a pull of the press or a lift of the winch, and they hold the load instead of letting it fall back. Vitruvius described a mill whose upright wheel drove a horizontal one through a row of teeth, and a marble saw driven the same way is known from a sarcophagus in Hierapolis."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "An ancient throat-and-girth strap pressed against a horse's windpipe the moment it leaned into a heavy load; the harder the animal pulled, the less it could breathe. Developed along the northern frontiers of China from earlier breast-strap harness and carried west across the steppe to medieval farms and roads, the padded rigid collar shifted the pull onto the shoulders, turning the horse into a fast, tireless draft partner for plow, mill and wagon alike."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "An emporion was neither a colony nor a city: a foothold on a foreign shore where the road met the sea and goods changed hands."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Ancient civilizations could count, but only now has knowledge moved from simple recipes to strict proofs. Geometry and abstract calculation let people not just watch the world but compute it: from the shape of a wheel's teeth to the motion of the stars."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Black stone that burns hotter and longer than firewood cropped out along hillsides and river cuts from northern China to Britain and the Meuse valley. Once miners drove deeper galleries with iron picks and windlasses, mineral coal fed city hearths, glasshouses, brew-kettles, stills, dye-vats and pottery kilns — though its biting, sulfurous smoke still spoiled bare iron in the furnace."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "By mixing fat with lye, people got soap, the first step toward hygiene and medicine."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Canals let people manage water flows, improving irrigation and opening new routes for trade."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Cheese kept milk for months, becoming an important winter supply."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Clay tablets became the first carrier of information, letting people pass knowledge across the centuries."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Clear glass alone gives back a feeble reflection. A foil of tin wetted with mercury and laid against its back gave an image no polished metal could match. The still supplied the mercury and the smelter the tin; the new craft taught glass to look back."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Draft animals let people harness animal strength for heavy work, greatly raising output and efficiency in farming and construction."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Dyeing cloth and pottery with various dyes is one of the oldest crafts."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Fermentation let people turn grain and fruit into alcohol, drunk in rituals and in daily life."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Fired clay became humanity's first artificial material, letting people store grain and water."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Fishing is one of humanity's oldest trades, and the first way to take food from the water."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "For centuries potters watched sand and alkali turn in their kilns into a shining crust of glass. Then one day someone had the idea of doing it on purpose. Glassmaking became more than an accident: it became a deliberate craft. The new material changed the world: beads, amulets, and later tableware and windows. Transparency became a symbol of enlightenment."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Hops made beer keep. Grain alone gave a drink that soured within days; a bittering charge of hops held it back, and beer became a good that could be stored and shipped."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "In scriptoriums scribes copied, translated, rewrote and recensioned texts on administration, the worship of the gods, magic and the curing of disease."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "India first boiled cane juice down to crystals that kept well, and the craft travelled with Arab growers to the warm edges of the Mediterranean."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Iron with a little carbon is another metal. Charcoal driven into a hot bloom and a flux that keeps the melt clean give a smith an edge that holds and a blade that does not bend."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Managing water flows became the key to claiming new lands: irrigation did more than water the fields, it drained waterlogged ground and turned marshes into fertile plains."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Mastering metals revolutionized the crafts: copper tools were tougher than stone."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Mead is the oldest alcohol, brewed from honey and water."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Parchment became an important carrier of information in the ancient world."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "People have tracked the turning of the seasons since the earliest times, but only now has the calendar become a standard tool of management: precise planning of sowing, pruning and harvest let plantations reach their full potential."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "People learned to alloy copper with tin to make bronze, a hard metal for tools and weapons. Bronze became a key material for crafts and warfare."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "People learned to draw stone and ore from the depths of the earth, opening a new era in tools."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "People learned to find and use native metals, which occur in nature in pure form, with no need to smelt them out of ore."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "People learned to tame bees for honey and wax, the only natural sweeteners and the stuff of candles."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "People noticed that the white crust on the shores of salt lakes cleanses and bleaches wonderfully. So began the extraction of soda, a valuable mineral without which neither glassmaking nor soapmaking is possible."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Plantations made it possible to grow olives and citrus in bulk, providing valuable goods and oils."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Raw coal ruins hot iron with sulfur and chokes a furnace with thick tarry smoke, yet the remedy lay in the charcoal burner's own craft. Baked slowly with little air until its volatile fumes burned off, stone coal turned into hard, porous grey coke — first charged into the iron furnaces of Song China when the surrounding hills ran short of timber, bearing heavier ore burdens than fragile wood charcoal ever could."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Raw leaf is harsh and spoils quickly. Cured and cut, it keeps for years, so curing is what turned a harvested leaf into a stored good."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Roasting minerals opens new possibilities for crafts: limestone yields lime, while roasted alunite is leached and crystallized to obtain alum for treating hides and fixing dyes."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Ropes became an important material for building, sailing and farming, letting people bind, haul and hoist loads."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Salt became an important product for preserving food."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Silk became a valuable material for clothing and trade, opening new possibilities for crafts and the economy."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Taming animals gave people a steady source of meat, milk and hides."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Tanning gave people a tough, flexible material for clothing, footwear and gear."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Tar sealed ships, ropes and leather, and it came both from a burning pine heap and from the bituminous ground."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "The amalgam mirror was bright, but its vapour sickened the room that made it and its film darkened with age. Chemistry offered a cleaner answer: silver dissolved in acid and laid down as a thin, even layer on clear glass. The reflection was truer, and it lasted."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "The art of exploring the land and drawing maps of it."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "The codex did not need to be unrolled: it opened at any page the reader chose and held the whole work between two covers. Sewing the sheets through the fold and gluing a tanned hide into a cover turned a sheaf of writing into a thing that could be shelved, carried and sold."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "The first farmers noticed that seeds thrown into the ground grew again. Thus began the age of settled life."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "The first step towards the modern glass industry."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "The grape is one of the oldest crops, from which people learned to make wine, a drink for feasts and rituals."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "The idea of value and exchange led to money, the basis of trade and city growth."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "The loom turned animal and plant fibers into cloth for garments and carpets."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "The perfumer's workshop held both goods at once: the resin ground with charcoal for the temple, and the resin dissolved in oil for the body. The people of the warm south had mixed the tears of the boswellia tree with oil long before anyone thought of distilling them."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "The skill of laying stone made it possible to build sturdy buildings that outlived centuries."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "The water wheel became an important source of power in the ancient world, automating the processing of grain and other materials."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "The wheel is one of humanity's greatest inventions, and it changed trade and war."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "The workshops knew the lever and the pulley long before anyone wrote them down. Heron of Alexandria set the pieces in order in his Mechanics, and later al-Khazini weighed the same pieces of rope and iron in the Book of the Balance of Wisdom. A machine, they wrote, is what moves a load we could not lift: a wheel on an axle, a crank, a connecting rod, a frame to hold it all. From this the hoist, the press and the mill stop being clever tricks and become craft — they can be built again, and then improved."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "This revolutionary invention made it possible to create an affordable, practical material for writing."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "To boil a liquid and to catch its breath in a cold vessel is to take the spirit of a thing and leave the thing behind. The still stood in the workshops of Alexandria and in the courts of the caliphate, and the physicians of Song China distilled the liquor of the millet. What came out of the alembic was worth more than what was poured into it."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Turning a millstone was only the first errand given to a river. Once cams and levers were fitted to the axle of the wheel, steady rotation became a measured stroke: Han ironmasters led streams to push the bellows of their furnaces day and night, ore stamps pounded vein-rock beside mountain sluices, and heavy trip hammers beat glowing blooms on the anvil without tiring."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Vegetable oil became an important food and a source of light."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Where cold water stands year after year, fallen moss and sedge do not rot away: they pack layer upon layer into dark, fibrous earth. Cut into wet bricks with a flat spade and stacked in the wind through summer, bog turf kept northern hearths warm and fired pots, brew-kettles and smokehouses wherever timber grew sparse."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Where rivers ran dry in summer or never crossed the plain at all, millers looked up instead of down. First on vertical axles behind wind-catching walls in Sistan and Khorasan, then on pivoting timber posts and stone towers whose cloth sails could be turned to face any breeze, the windmill brought tireless grinding to dry uplands and open coasts alike."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Wine was made from grapes: it was a drink for feasts and rituals, and it also kept the perishable grapes from spoiling."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Wood is the oldest building material, and with the first axes people learned to fell trees and saw the trunks into planks."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Working precious stones and metals turned the craft into art."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "An oil lamp spills when carried into a gallery or held beside a ledger. A wick drawn repeatedly through melted tallow or rolled in warm beeswax carries its own fuel in solid form: tallow burns cheaply down in the mine shafts, while clear beeswax lights scriptoriums, temples and benches without stinging smoke."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Long before anyone caught metallic zinc in a vessel, smelters sealed copper with crushed calamine and charcoal in a closed crucible and drew out a golden metal that neither rusted like iron nor needed scarce tin. From Persian birinj and Mediterranean orichalcum to the cast plaques of West Africa and the astrolabes of astronomers, brass became the alloy of fine scales, vessels and enduring ornament."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Not every forest yields a strong lye: resinous pines leave a thin ash, so ash-burners sought out beech, maple, oak, chestnut and walnut, burned great stacks of seasoned timber, leached the ashes through water and calcined the liquor in hot pots into white potash. Costlier and slower to win than the crust of a soda lake, it freed inland workshops from distant natron and gave glassmakers, dyers and nitre-boilers an alkali of their own."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Alchemists searching for elixirs in Tang and Song China ground saltpeter with sulfur and charcoal and named the sudden roar fire-medicine. Carried west along the trade roads through Persian, Arab and European workshops, the black grain did more than reshape battlefields: tamped into a drilled hole, it split in a breath the granite and vein-rock that picks and wedges had worried for weeks."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Where the alchemist sought a secret in the smoke of one vessel, the chemist weighed what went in and weighed what came out. Sulfur burned under moist bells into oil of vitriol, sulfuric acid drove fuming spirits of salt and nitre cleanly from common salts, clay yielded alum without alunite stone, and measured charges of potash and lead cleared glass into crystal. Matter ceased to be a riddle of sympathies and became a craft of proportions."
msgstr ""

