msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Banking-like practices took different forms across history. In the ancient Near East, Mesopotamian temples made interest-bearing loans from their treasuries; Greek trapezitai at money-changing tables handled exchange and credit, while Roman argentarii managed payments, credit and exchange. Indian inscriptions record interest-bearing endowments entrusted to śreṇi guilds. In Tang China, guifang offered safekeeping services and feiqian certificates facilitated transfers. These were not modern banks, but their distinct services helped people manage wealth and move value farther than coin alone."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "technologies.flavor"
msgid "Knowledge kept on one shelf dies with the man who read it. Teaching makes it survive: a master reads aloud, pupils copy, question and correct, and the next generation begins where the last one stopped. Academies in China, madrasas, monastery schools and houses of learning in Africa and India kept that same cycle in their own way. A university is what a scriptorium becomes when copying gives way to teaching."
msgstr ""

