msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Counting people is among the oldest acts of government: Egyptian grain enumerations, Chinese household registers and Inca quipu records all listed residents so that rations and dues could be shared out. The heads counted were usually households, not individuals, and the list was rewritten as families grew or moved.\n\nThe notion that every resident draws a common share, employed or idle, runs through many traditions — from communal grain distributions to the poll assessments of market towns. Keeping such a roll turned an abstract population into a body the town could provision and tax."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Digging sticks and hoes cultivated ground millennia before the plough. Early farmers worked floodplains and clearings by hand: maize, beans and squash in Mesoamerica, rice in Asian wetlands, millet and sorghum in Africa, wheat in West Asia. Such plots demanded weeding and water control, not draft power.\n\nThe animal-drawn ard let a household break harder soil and cover more ground; iron shares later eased the work. Where draft animals were scarce, intensive hand cultivation persisted — Asian wet-rice terraces, garden plots elsewhere — making the plough one option among many, not a single path."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Herding grew wherever grazing animals were domesticated: sheep and goats in West Asia, cattle in Africa and South Asia, yaks on the Tibetan plateau, llamas in the Andes, reindeer in northern Eurasia. Herders moved with their animals to pasture and water, protecting them and counting heads at the fold.\n\nSeasons shaped the work — summer uplands, winter lowlands, long treks between. Some communities stayed mobile, trading their products; others folded the flock into village life. The herd was a walking store of food, fibre and fuel, its size counted in an economy of living animals."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Keeping poultry and small stock around the house needed no pasture, only scraps and shelter. Chickens descend from wild fowl of South and Southeast Asia and spread through village yards worldwide; ducks were domesticated in Asia, guinea fowl in Africa, turkeys in Mesoamerica. Every region had its own barnyard bird.\n\nBirds that foraged and bred in a yard made this work a household task beside gardening and cooking. As flocks grew, keepers added coops and selective breeding, turning a few scavengers into a steady supply of eggs, meat and feathers close to the door."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Pigs were domesticated independently in West and East Asia and spread across Asia, Africa and the Pacific islands wherever they could be fed. Omnivorous rooters, they thrived on scraps, so a sty beside the house needed no open pasture.\n\nAcross Asia and Oceania pigs were raised for feasts and counted as wealth; elsewhere they supplied a household with meat and lard. Herders drove them to woodland for mast, fattened them on kitchen waste and later penned them in sties, turning a scavenger into a managed animal."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Mining followed useful stone and metal below the surface: turquoise and copper in West Asia, tin in Central Asia, gold in Nubia and the Andes. Before explosives, picks and hammers broke rock, or fire-setting heated a face until water cracked it.\n\nDeep galleries required timber props, ventilation and drainage, and work was organized around shifts and haulage. Ore was sorted and crushed at the mouth, then sent to furnaces. Tools changed, but the sequence — locate a seam, follow it underground, hold the roof up, lift the rock out — was much the same everywhere."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Open quarries supplied stone for tools, walls and roads long before deep shafts were needed. Flint came from pits in many regions; limestone, sandstone, granite and marble were worked at surface outcrops. Egyptian temple blocks, Inca masonry and dressed stone in African and Asian towns all began as cuts in rock.\n\nQuarrymen split stone with wedges and hammers, levered blocks free and dragged them away. Growing settlements turned scattered collecting into organized cuttings with tool shops and transport routes. The methods stayed conservative, but the scale and the distance blocks travelled grew enormously."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Wild nests were robbed before bees were kept, and keeping arose independently in many lands: clay hives in West Asia and Egypt, woven or log hives across Africa and Asia, stingless bees tended by the Maya. Any seasonal bloom could support a few hives.\n\nColonies could be moved to follow flowering, so the craft travelled with its owner, not to one field. Baskets and hollow logs gave way to hives with movable parts, letting keepers inspect the brood and take honey without killing the colony. Wax and honey travelled far in trade."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Wild grapes were gathered first; cultivation took hold in the Caucasus and West Asia, where the oldest evidence of winemaking comes from, and spread with trade across Eurasia. Vineyards trained vines on the ground, on trellises or on shading pergolas.\n\nDressing vines was a yearly cycle of pruning, tying and weeding, then one concentrated harvest. Since the fruit ripened together, the work needed many hands in a few weeks. Grapes could be eaten fresh, dried or pressed, so the dresser's skill lay in timing and care, not heavy machinery."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Clay was dug wherever settlements lay near usable deposits: river silt in Mesopotamia and on the Nile, loess in China, weathered rock in Africa. Raw clay is seldom workable, so diggers weathered it in frost and rain, then soaked and kneaded it, letting grit settle out.\n\nKnowing which seam would fire without cracking was a skill handed down alongside the digging. As towns grew, clay getting became a separate trade serving potters, brick makers and tablet scribes. The tools stayed simple — pick, basket, settling pit — but the scale grew from a household pit to whole workshops."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "In waterlogged ground, dead plants build up as peat. Turf cutting for fuel is an old practice in the cool bogs of northern Eurasia and North America, though its earliest dates are uncertain — turf leaves little trace. Where wood was scarce, a bog could heat a household.\n\nCutters stripped the surface, sliced wet turf with a spade and stacked it in rows to dry. Dry peat burned slowly and evenly, but winning it took space and labour. Hand cutting later gave way to drainage, machines and briquettes — the fuel stayed the same; only gathering changed."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Settlements formed near springs, and drawing water was among the first town tasks. Catchments took many forms: stone basins in West Asia, stepwells in South Asia, cenotes in Mesoamerica, channels in the Andes. Mineral springs, tasting of salts, were prized for healing and drew crowds.\n\nWater was carried in jars; catchments and gutters saved trips, then pipes and carts brought it nearer. Since a polluted well could sicken a quarter, keepers of springs and conduits held a responsible post. The work shifted from carrying to managing a supply, yet the spring kept its value."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Vessels were shaped by hand for millennia before the wheel: pinched, coiled, smoothed, then fired in an open fire or pit. Wheel and kiln appeared at different times in West Asia, the Indus valley, Egypt and China, letting one potter throw thin walls and fire wares evenly.\n\nThe wheel was never universal. Across the Americas, Africa and Oceania, potters coiled and burnished fine ceramics without a wheel or kiln, their work no less skilled. Where wheels spread, the craft favored speed and uniform output — pots, bricks, tablets — while hand methods continued for special wares."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Farmers grew crops for fibre, colour and scent: cotton in the Indus valley and Mesoamerica, ramie in East Asia, pepper in South Asia, indigo in Africa, tobacco and cacao in the Americas. Many were used locally long before travelling far; some crossed continents only after contact.\n\nThese crops demanded more care than grain: seedbeds, transplanting, weeding and a dry place to cure the harvest. Their value lay in exchange, so growers were tied to markets and traders. The work — seed, tending, drying — changed little, but the range of plants and the distances they travelled grew."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Tar reaches the surface on its own: bitumen seeps in Mesopotamia, asphalt rises at Trinidad's Pitch Lake, conifers bleed resin across Eurasia and North America. People used it as glue and sealant long before kilns; Mesopotamian builders bonded bricks with natural bitumen.\n\nGathering seepage and resin was simple; boiling drove off water and thickened it into pitch for caulking boats. With growing demand, collectors turned to destructive distillation — heating wood in a closed pit to make tar and charcoal. The source shifted from a seep to a furnace, but the uses stayed the same."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Alkali salts form where water evaporates from closed basins: natron in Egypt, trona in East Africa, deposits in dry inland Asia. The same chemistry also came from plants — salt-tolerant species were burned and their ash soaked for carbonate — a practice known around the Mediterranean and across Asia.\n\nGathering crust or ash was only the start: it had to be dissolved, strained and evaporated for use. Glassmakers used it to melt sand, soapmakers to cut fat, dyers to fix colour. For centuries supply followed the deposits and plants; factory-made soda freed the trade from those places."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Fish were taken long before boats: bone hooks, woven nets and spears appear at coastal sites everywhere. Outrigger canoes in the Pacific, dhows in the Indian Ocean, junks in East Asia and kayaks in the Arctic carried fishers beyond the surf to grounds the shore could not reach.\n\nA dock made the boat a tool: a place to land, mend nets and cure the catch. Hulls and sails improved, voyages lengthened, and dried or salted fish went inland. The craft adapted to local waters, but its rhythm of setting, hauling and preserving stayed the same."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Felling trees with stone and later metal axes is among humanity's oldest crafts; people cut timber for fuel, shelter and boats long before any written record, on every forested continent from the Amazon to Siberia. The work demanded seasonal camps, skids, rafts and teams hauling logs to water or workshops. Over time the axe gave way to the two-man saw and, much later, mechanical saws, but the essential pattern — select, fell, drag, float — stayed remarkably constant across regions."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Charcoal making is nearly as old as controlled fire: wood heated slowly with little air drives off water and tar, leaving a light, hot-burning fuel. The technique appears independently wherever metalworkers needed high heat — in African and Asian furnaces as well as European forests — usually in conical pits or earth-covered mounds tended for days. Burners watched smoke colour and sealed cracks to control air. Even after coal and coke displaced charcoal in heavy industry, the same craft survived for forge fuel and cooking."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Glass first emerged where sand, an alkali and sufficient heat met; the earliest vessels and beads come from Egypt and Mesopotamia, and glassmaking independently took root in China and elsewhere. Craftsmen melted the batch in crucibles or tank furnaces, then shaped it by winding around a core, casting, or — a later innovation — blowing through a tube. The trade travelled widely with migrant workshops, and its recipes shifted from natron to plant ash depending on local supplies, so each region produced its own characteristic glass."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Extracting metal from ore by heat is an ancient, widespread art: copper was reduced from its ores in Anatolia, the Balkans, the Andes and Africa, each tradition developing its own furnace and flux. Smelters built clay shafts or bowl furnaces, layered ore with charcoal, and tapped the resulting metal or bloom. The basic chemistry changed little for millennia, though bellows, water power and larger furnaces steadily raised output. What changed most was scale, not the fundamental act of separating metal from stone."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Once metal could be won from ore, shaping it by hammering followed almost everywhere: smiths in Africa, Asia, Europe and the Americas forged tools, weapons and ornaments at a charcoal hearth. The craft demanded judgement of heat and hammer blow, and it absorbed new metals as they arrived — bronze, iron, steel, brass. Smiths often doubled as repairers and traders, their portable skill travelling with caravans and settlements. Mechanised rolling and casting later took over bulk production, yet hand forging persisted where a strong, custom piece was needed."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Before modern laboratories, what passed for science often meant the copying, comparing and commenting on texts — work done in temples, monasteries, madrasas and courts across Asia, Africa and Europe. Scholars preserved astronomical tables, medical recipes and legal lore, adding observations of their own. Institution-building varied: some traditions centred on a single royal observatory, others on networks of scribes. The printing press and later the experimental method widened the circle, but the core activity — careful recording and critical reading of inherited knowledge — remained recognisable."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Coins appeared independently in several regions — Lydia, China, and the Indian subcontinent among them — as stamped metal pieces whose mark guaranteed weight and purity. Mint workers weighed and assayed bullion, cast or cut blanks, and struck them between dies; the ruler's image turned a lump of metal into trusted money. Techniques spread by trade and conquest, from hammered flakes to milled edges. Debasement and re-striking were recurring temptations, so the assay and the stamp were as much political as technical acts."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Organised taxation is as old as cities: ancient Egypt, Mesopotamia, China and the Inca state all kept censuses and assessed dues in grain, labour or coin. Collectors tallied households and harvests, assessed obligations and handed the proceeds to the treasury, often farming the task out to contractors who kept a cut. Methods ranged from tally sticks and papyrus rolls to quipu knots in the Andes. The underlying need — a reliable count of people and produce — outlasted every change of currency or regime."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Fermenting grape juice into wine is an ancient art, with evidence of production in the Caucasus, the Near East and the Mediterranean, and independent traditions wherever grapes grew. Makers crushed fruit, pressed the must and let natural yeasts work in clay vessels, later in wooden casks; the container shaped flavour as much as the grape. The craft spread with trade and colonisation across continents, and wherever settlers carried vines they rebuilt presses and cellars. Techniques evolved from skin-fermented jars to controlled fermentation, yet the basic sequence — crush, press, ferment, age — endures."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Beer-like fermented grain drinks were brewed independently in Mesopotamia, Egypt, China and the Americas, often before bread and beer parted ways as separate crafts. Brewers mashed crushed grain with water, sometimes adding hops or other bittering plants, boiled the wort and let it ferment in cool vessels. In many regions brewing was a household or temple activity; later it became a specialised trade with proprietary recipes. The arrival of temperate grains, reliable refrigeration and industrial yeast transformed scale and consistency, but the mash-and-ferment core is ancient."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Pressing oil from seeds and fruit is a widespread ancient technology: olives in the Mediterranean, sesame and linseed in Asia and Africa, and later groundnuts and maize in the Americas. Makers crushed the material under edge mills or mortars and squeezed it in beam or screw presses, sometimes heating or leaching to raise yield. Oil served food, lamp light, soap and ritual. The screw press and, much later, hydraulic and expeller presses increased output, yet the essential idea — crush and squeeze — remained the same."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Perfumery grew from the preparation of aromatic resins, oils and flowers for ritual, medicine and adornment — practices documented in Egypt, India, Arabia, China and among Indigenous peoples of the Americas. Makers ground, steeped, macerated or distilled fragrant material, fixing scents in fats, oils or alcohol. Distillation improved and expanded the palette of essential oils; alcohol-based perfumes spread widely through trade. The desire to capture and preserve scent is near-universal, and the same techniques were reinvented in many places with local flowers, woods and resins."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Colouring textiles is an ancient, widespread craft: indigo in Asia, Africa and the Americas, madder and safflower across Eurasia, cochineal in the Americas, and many local roots and barks. Dyers prepared baths, used mordants such as alum or tannin to fix colour, and layered dips to build shades. Recipes were closely guarded and varied by region and available plants. The arrival of synthetic dyes in the nineteenth century displaced many natural ones, but for millennia the dyer's skill lay in knowing which local material would yield a fast, repeatable colour."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Weaving predates recorded history; fragments of woven cloth survive from neolithic sites in the Near East, Asia and the Americas, and the loom was developed independently in many regions. Weavers interlaced warp and weft threads on frames ranging from simple backstrap looms to large treadle machines, producing plain cloth, patterned textiles and carpets. The craft supported entire households and economies — cloth served as currency and tribute in places such as the Andes. Mechanisation later transformed output in some regions, yet hand weaving endures wherever patterned, local fabric is valued."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Paper was invented in China, where craftsmen beat mulberry and other fibres into pulp, suspended them in water and lifted thin sheets on a screened frame; the technique spread through Asia to the Islamic world and beyond. Makers cooked and macerated plant material, formed sheets, pressed and dried them, producing a cheap, light writing surface that gradually displaced costlier materials. Mills using water power and, later, machines for pulping and forming mass-produced paper transformed scale. The essential operation — fibre in water, sheet on a screen — remained unchanged for centuries."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Writing skins were prepared wherever livestock were raised: hides were soaked in lime, scraped clean of hair and flesh, stretched on a frame and dried into a smooth, durable surface. This craft is documented in Egypt and across Eurasia, with regional variants in the treatment of skins from calf, goat and sheep. Parchment was costly and labour-intensive, reserved for important texts, and its quality depended on careful scraping and stretching. Paper eventually displaced it for everyday writing, but parchment remained the medium of choice for formal and illuminated manuscripts."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Bound books arose in several traditions at once: Roman scribes sewed folded quires, Coptic binders stitched them into leather boards, and East Asian makers folded and stapled sheets into their own formats. The codex won out because it could be carried, shelved and opened at any page.\n\nEarly binders sewed sheets onto cords and glued the spine beneath a cover; presses and tooled decoration came later. Workshops followed literacy, paper and trade routes, so the craft travelled with schools and merchants rather than belonging to one empire."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Turning raw hide into stable leather is ancient and widespread: Egyptian and Mesopotamian workshops used vegetable tannins, while peoples of the Americas and the Pacific chewed or smoked skins and used fats and brains. Mineral tanning with alum and chrome came much later.\n\nThe trade was often kept outside town walls because of its smell and foul water. Tanners soaked hides in lime, worked them with bark or salts, then scraped and stretched them. Leather then moved into saddlery, footwear, bookbinding and harness wherever animals were raised."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Woodworking is among the oldest crafts: chopping, splitting and joining timber needed only stone and copper tools. Carpenters raised frames, bridges, boats and tools in every region where trees grew, from the Nile to the forests of East Asia and the Americas.\n\nThe toolkit changed slowly — axes, adzes, saws, chisels and pegs did most of the work for millennia. Joinery that locked pieces without nails let serious structures stand. Carpenter's work was the framework beneath nearly every other trade: houses, carts, looms, ploughs and the hulls of ships."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Coopery grew wherever goods had to travel in bulk: fitted staves bound by hoops made a vessel that was light, strong and repairable. Ancient Egyptian and Mesopotamian scenes show wooden casks, and the craft is documented in early China and among peoples of the Americas.\n\nA cooper cut staves, heated them to bend, then drove hoops tight so the joints swelled shut. Sealed with tar or resin, the barrel carried water, wine, oil, grain and salted fish. Its portability made it a partner of trade and shipping long before metal containers existed."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Furniture began as few movable pieces: a stool, a mat, a chest. Examples survive from ancient Egypt and China, and from the Americas, where low seats and woven furnishings suited life on the floor. As houses grew more permanent, joinery produced beds, tables and storage.\n\nThe craft balanced comfort, status and available timber. Makers in different regions found their own solutions — lacquered wood in East Asia, inlaid and carved pieces in the Islamic world, joined oak in the north. Luxury furniture followed the same trade routes as the materials and tastes behind it."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Distillation — boiling a liquid and cooling its vapour — was known in ancient Mesopotamia and Egypt for perfumes and medicines, and independently in Asia. The still spread long before strong drink became its main use, so the technique predates any single spirit.\n\nEarly stills were pots with a cooled lid; later designs added long condensers and repeated runs. Distillers learned to discard the tail and redistil for purity. Spirits, solvents and medicinal waters came from the same apparatus, and the craft followed grain, fruit and sugar wherever they grew."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Alchemists worked across continents: Chinese and Indian practitioners sought elixirs and transmutations, Hellenistic and Islamic scholars built a huge corpus of laboratory practice, and similar pursuits existed in other traditions. Much of practical chemistry grew out of this search, whatever its mystical goals.\n\nRetorts, alembics, gentle heat and careful weighing let them distil acids, solvents and salts. Recipes were guarded and often written obscurely, so knowledge moved through apprenticeship and trade. When the search for gold faded, the same skills fed pharmacy, metallurgy and the growing study of matter."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Jewellery is older than cities: beads of shell, bone and stone appear worldwide, and gold was worked in ancient Egypt, Nubia and the Americas long before contact between them. Few tools were needed, so the craft arose wherever metal and stone could be found.\n\nEarly jewelers hammered, cast, soldered and set stones with punches and bow drills; finer abrasives came later. Pieces served as adornment, currency and grave goods, marking rank. Styles travelled with merchants, and each region developed its own grammar of metal, colour and form."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Soap arose independently in several places: Babylonian tablets describe boiling fats with alkali, and similar recipes appear in ancient Egypt and China. Plant ashes and animal fat were enough, so the craft did not wait for any single civilisation to discover it.\n\nEarly soap was a soft cleansing paste for washing and wool; cheap kinds served fullers and dyers before household use spread. The process changed little for centuries: fat or oil boiled with lye, then salted out and cut. Regions used olive oil, sesame, coconut or tallow, giving local soaps their own character."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Candles grew from the simple fact that fat and wax burn with a wick. Clay lamps and rushlights lit homes long before dipped candles; both moulds and dipping appear in ancient Egypt and China and later Islamic lands. No rare material was needed, so the craft arose in many places.\n\nTallow gave a cheap, smoky light, beeswax burned cleaner and cost more; resin and plant waxes found local use. Chandlers dipped wicks, poured moulds and stored candles against dark seasons. Their trade linked the hive, the animal and the household, later church and workshop."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Stone dressing is prehistoric: hammerstones shaped rough blocks, and communities from the Nile to the Andes raised walls and tombs with them. Mortar was not universal — dry-stone work in many regions needed only skill and patience, while others used mud, lime or gypsum.\n\nMasons quarried, squared and set stone, cutting joints so weight passed cleanly into the wall. Tools changed slowly: bronze and iron chisels, wedges and levers did most of the work for ages. Their craft built houses, bridges, aqueducts and temples, and its proportions were often sacred knowledge passed between masters."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Preserving food with smoke and drying is ancient and widespread: fish and meat were hung over slow fires in North America, Siberia, Africa and Southeast Asia, each with its own fuels and methods. Only a fire and time were needed, so no single culture can claim the discovery.\n\nSmoke dried the surface, added flavour and helped keep insects and spoilage away; cold smoking treated fish without cooking. Smokers learned to choose woods, control a low fire and judge the season. Cured fish and meat travelled far, feeding households, ships and winter camps long before refrigeration."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Grinding grain between stones is among humanity's oldest operations; hand querns appear worldwide from early farming onward. Water-driven mills are documented in the Hellenistic world and later in China, India and the Islamic world, while windmills developed separately in several regions.\n\nThe miller's skill lay in tending the stones, dressing their furrows and regulating grain and water. Mills were community property as much as businesses, and their owners often held a local monopoly. Whether turned by hand, animal, stream or wind, mills stood where grain was grown."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Sails turned cloth into a machine for catching wind, and the idea is old: Egyptian and Mesopotamian boats carried linen sails, while Pacific and Asian traditions made mats and cloth of their own. Early sails were woven near the shore, so weaver and sailmaker were often one person.\n\nSailmakers cut panels, sewed strong seams, hemmed the edges and treated the cloth with oil or tar against water and rot. They worked to the hull's shape and the wind's strength. The craft followed trade routes and shipyards, and its patterns changed as rigs grew complex."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Boats were built independently wherever waterways mattered: dugouts, reed bundles, skin frames and planked hulls appear in Africa, the Americas, Asia and the Pacific, often without contact. The problem was always the same — a frame light enough to float and stiff enough to carry.\n\nBuilders shaped timber, lashed or pegged the frame, added planks and sealed seams with tar, resin or clay; some traditions used no metal. Designs reflected local waters: shallow rivers, open coasts or islands. The craft travelled with trade, and each region kept its own way of joining a hull."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "professions.flavor"
msgid "Butchery is as old as domestication: once animals were kept, killing and dividing the carcass followed. Stone and bone knives preceded metal, and customs differed — halal and kosher rules, pig butchery in East Asia, llama and camel butchery in the Andes and Central Asia.\n\nButchers bled and dressed the animal, split the hide, removed organs and cut meat into pieces for cooking, drying or salting. Nothing was wasted: fat became tallow, skin became leather, bones and sinew served other trades. The work was often done outdoors, shaped by custom, season and household need."
msgstr ""


msgctxt "improvements.flavor"
msgid "A camp in the forest is among the first workshops of a settled community. Where a valuable tree grows, the camp takes that timber; where the forest is ordinary, it cuts wood by the density of the stand. It does not clear the land for good — it follows the forest as it regrows."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "improvements.flavor"
msgid "A harbor is not a shelter for one ship but a claim on a whole body of water. Once the quay exists, every shoal and landing place on that lake or sea becomes reachable, and each body of water needs its own. The harbor itself needs no worker: it is the place from which other workers set out."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "improvements.flavor"
msgid "A spring is a small but dependable gift, easily muddied by people and animals. A catchment built around it guards the water and lets it be drawn in quantity. The masonry is simple, yet it is what turns a trickle into a supply the town can count on."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "improvements.flavor"
msgid "Beekeeping is known from many ancient societies, from the Nile valley to the forests of Eastern Europe and the islands of the Pacific. Keepers learned to move hives to the flowering season instead of chasing wild swarms. The apiary turns the blossoms of the whole neighbourhood into honey and wax without taking the land out of other use."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "improvements.flavor"
msgid "Canals are among the oldest pieces of infrastructure: a dug channel carries water further than anyone can carry a bucket. Once a chain of canals exists, water passes from one field to the next and every farm along it benefits. The canal needs no worker of its own — its work is done by gravity."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "improvements.flavor"
msgid "Crops such as sugarcane, cotton and tea need a long season and a steady labour force. A plantation gathers them on one estate and processes them near the field, because the raw plants lose value quickly after harvest. Once irrigation is understood, the same estate can carry water to its neighbours."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "improvements.flavor"
msgid "Fishing boats extend the town beyond the shoreline. They can be used only where a harbor already gives access to the water, for a boat needs somewhere to land and a community to mend it. The catch comes from the water rather than the field, so the boats belong to a different economy than the farm."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "improvements.flavor"
msgid "Grapes grow readily in many climates and were pressed long before the plant was given rows and trellises. A vineyard fixes the crop in one place, so the harvest can be timed and its quality watched from year to year. The same fruit is eaten fresh or fermented, and the two uses compete for one harvest."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "improvements.flavor"
msgid "In cold wetlands, dead plant matter accumulates faster than it decays and forms peat. It can be cut by hand, dried and burned, and it served as fuel wherever wood was scarce. A peat pit is a mine of a different kind: its ore is the slow growth of a bog."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "improvements.flavor"
msgid "Keeping herds on open land is older than permanent fields. From the steppes of Central Asia to the savannas of Africa and the pampas of South America, herders learned to follow the grasses rather than sow them. A pasture fixes that wandering practice in one place and makes it part of the town's economy."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "improvements.flavor"
msgid "Natural soda was gathered from dry lake beds and alkaline springs long before factories made it. It serves in washing, in glassmaking and in the preparation of food. A soda quarry is the place where those deposits are concentrated enough to be worked."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "improvements.flavor"
msgid "Not every domesticated animal can live on a range. Poultry, pigeons and rabbits do better in an enclosed yard, and a fenced homestead describes their place better than a grazing field. The small enclosure gives a household meat and manure without a larger territory."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "improvements.flavor"
msgid "Pigs were domesticated independently in several regions and are kept close to the dwelling rather than driven to pasture. A sty keeps them out of the crops and turns household scraps into meat and fertilizer. It is a small structure with a large return."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "improvements.flavor"
msgid "Pottery appears wherever clay, water and fire meet, and it begins without a stone kiln. The pit supplies the earth; the potter decides what it becomes. Because clay is heavy and fragile in transit, the potter's house is almost always built beside the pit."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "improvements.flavor"
msgid "Stone is the oldest building material that does not rot. A quarry supplies the blocks for walls, roads and foundations, and its workers learn to read the grain of the rock before they cut it. Unlike a mine, it works at the surface and needs no shafts."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "improvements.flavor"
msgid "Tar and pitch were known wherever coniferous forests grew: the resin was heated until it ran and then used to seal boats and roofs. Burning it in a pit is a simple operation, but it needs a steady supply of wood and a worker who knows when to stop the fire. The product is a waterproof layer that outlasts the timber it protects."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "improvements.flavor"
msgid "The first mines followed veins visible at the surface; deeper workings waited on timber supports, drainage and reliable lighting. Knowledge of rock and ore passed from master to apprentice in every mining region, from the Andes to the Urals and the hills of East Asia. A mine is where that knowledge becomes a lasting source of metal."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "improvements.flavor"
msgid "Where the sun is strong and the sea or a salt lake is near, a shallow pond makes salt by evaporation alone. No fire and no machinery are needed — only a basin, a season and patience. Salt preserves food, and a dependable pond frees the town from distant mines."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "improvements.flavor"
msgid "Wherever grains were first domesticated — in the river valleys of South Asia, in the Americas, in the Near East — the same operation appeared: break the soil and return seed to it. A farm does not change the land; it gives a settled community a dependable way to work the food plants already growing there."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A kiln that holds a steady, fierce fire turns a household craft into a workshop trade. Such kilns were built in East Asia and West Asia, where potters learned to read the flame and mix mineral glazes; better draught and a charge that does not spoil let one potter fire far more ware in a day."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Grinding grain between stones needs no engine, only a heavy upper stone and a hand to turn it. Hand mills appear wherever people cultivate grain, from the river valleys of South Asia to the highlands of the Americas, and remained in use long after larger mills existed. The small mill lets a household do without the workshop down the road."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Once draft animals were trained to walk a circle, the same millstones could be turned without human effort. The animal mill carries a heavier stone than the hand mill and frees the household's time for other work. It is a change of power, not of the principle of grinding."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A wheel in a stream turns the grindstone without tiring people or animals, but it needs a channel and a fall of water. Water mills were built in many regions, from the valleys of China to the river towns of the Middle East, wherever a reliable current could be led to the stone. The mill ties the town's bread to the rhythm of the water."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Where water is scarce but wind is steady, sails can drive the same millstones. The cloth sail is light, cheap to replace and quick to mend, so a windmill can stand where a water wheel cannot. Its two sets of stones let one building serve more than one batch of grain."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Bread needs only flour, water and fire, yet keeping a steady oven hot is work in itself. Communal ovens are known from the earliest farming towns in many parts of the world; a bakery makes that shared hearth a permanent place in the city. It turns the staple grain into a food that travels and keeps."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Butchery is as old as domestication: once animals are kept for milk or wool, their meat, hide and bones must eventually be processed. A slaughterhouse gathers that work in one place so that nothing of the animal is wasted. It links the pasture to the tanner, the soap maker and the kitchen."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Milk spoils quickly, but cheese and other cultured milk foods keep for months. Herders developed the techniques independently from the steppes to the highlands of East Africa. A dairy makes those keeping qualities available to the whole town."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Wood burns hotter and cleaner when it is first turned to charcoal in a sealed kiln. The operation is simple enough to be known wherever wood was plentiful, and it made metalworking possible with a small amount of fuel. A kiln turns a bulky forest product into one a smith can carry."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Coal can be cleaned of smoke and sulfur by heating it without air, much as wood is turned to charcoal. The resulting coke burns hot enough to reduce iron ore in a shaft furnace. That is why the coke oven belongs to the age of large-scale metal production rather than the first forge."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A forge is where heated metal is shaped by hammering. It needs a hearth, a hammer and a worker who knows when the metal is soft enough — knowledge passed on in every metalworking tradition. The tools made here are what all the other crafts depend on."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A water-driven hammer strikes without tiring a smith, so larger pieces of metal can be worked in one session. It is the same principle as the water mill, applied to a hammer instead of a grindstone. The workshop lets one smith do the work of several."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Ore is not yet metal: it must be heated with fuel until the metal separates from the rock. Smelting is known from very early metalworking cultures in Africa, Asia and the Americas, each with its own furnace design. A smelter repeats that transformation day after day."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A bloomery reduces iron ore below the melting point, leaving a spongy mass of iron that is hammered into shape. Furnaces of this kind were built across many regions, from the Andes to West Africa and East Asia. They supply metal before a taller furnace becomes possible."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A tall shaft furnace reaches the temperature at which iron melts, so the metal can be tapped as a liquid and cast. It needs a steady blast of air, a charge of ore and flux, and a furnace that stands repeated heat. The blast furnace turns iron from a craft material into a bulk one."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "The potter's wheel shapes clay faster and more evenly than hands alone. Wheels were used in many ancient centres of pottery, from the Yangtze valley to the Near East, and the potter's house turns that skill into the vessels the town needs. Fired clay also gives the builder brick and tile."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Stone must be cut to size before it can be built into a wall. A workshop with chisels and a yard for blocks lets one mason prepare stone for many building sites. Since stone is heavy, the work is done as near the quarry and the town as possible."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A hide left untreated soon decays; tanning with bark or other plant matter turns it into leather that lasts. The craft is found wherever animals are raised, from the steppes to the savannas. The tanner's house supplies shoes, straps and covers to the rest of the city."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A loom turns thread into cloth, crossing one thread after another. Simple looms were known to farmers and herders in many regions — flax in the north, cotton in South Asia, ramie in East Asia, wool on the steppes. The house keeps that daily work inside the town."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A foot treadle lifts the shed of the loom, leaving both hands free to pass the shuttle. Several such looms under one roof let different cloths be woven at the same time. The workshop increases output without changing the fibre or the weave."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Fermentation turns grain into a low-alcohol drink that keeps better than the mash it came from. Brewing is known in many farming societies, from the Nile valley to the highlands of East Africa and the valleys of Asia. The brewer uses the same grain the miller and the baker use."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Large vats let several batches ferment at once, each with its own time and temperature. The vessels are simple, but the workshop needs a worker who can judge when a batch is ready. The same skill turns the drink into vinegar when it is left too long."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Grapes begin to ferment on their own once their skins are broken, so pressing is the oldest part of the craft. Vineyards and wild grapes are found in many climates, and the drink travelled far beyond the region where the fruit grew. The winemaker's house turns a perishable harvest into a good that can be stored."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "The seeds of many plants hold oil that can be pressed out with a simple lever or wedge. Sesame in Africa and Asia, olives around the Mediterranean, and oil crops of the Americas all gave rise to the same craft. The house supplies cooking fat, the base for soap and fuel for lamps."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A wedge press applies more force than a hand can, so more oil is recovered from the same batch of seed. The mill keeps several presses at work, each serving a batch of its own. It is the workshop version of the operation the oilmaker's house performs."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Glass is made by melting sand with a flux, and it can be blown into shape while hot. The craft appeared in several ancient regions and travelled with the workers who knew the recipe. A glassmaker's house gives the town vessels and windows that clay and wood cannot."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Rope is made by twisting fibres together; the twist is what gives a bundle of strands its strength. Fibres differ by region — hemp, flax, jute, agave — but the operation is the same. Every ship, well and building site needs the product of this house."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A long covered lane lets a worker twist a rope to its full length in one pass. The building is long, but the tools are simple, and the result is a cable strong enough for a sail or a hoist. A ropewalk supplies the shipyard and the windmill alike."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Tar and pitch are made by heating resinous wood until the tar runs out. The same product sealed boats and roofs in many regions, from the northern forests to the pine woods of the Americas. The distillery gathers the whole process in one workshop."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Soap is made by combining fat with alkali, and both are at hand wherever animals are rendered and soda or ash is available. The recipe was known in ancient Mesopotamia, in Egypt and in many later traditions. The house turns otherwise useless scraps into a good the whole town uses."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Silk comes from the cocoon of a moth, which must be reeled before the insect breaks it. Sericulture began in East Asia and spread through trade to other regions; raising the worms and weaving the thread are two different crafts. The house turns that thread into cloth."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Limestone heated in a kiln becomes quicklime. In the surrounding yard, workers slake the lime and mix it with sand for mortar, or with reactive volcanic material and stone aggregate for concrete. Firing and mixing are separate operations within the same works. The kiln also processes gypsum and alunite into materials for other workshops."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Papyrus is made by laying strips of the reed's stem in crossing layers and pressing them into a sheet. The craft is tied to a plant that grows in warm wetlands and gave writing a light, cheap surface. The workshop turns a water plant into the material of records."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Smoking preserves fish by drying it and coating it with compounds from the smoke. Coastal and river peoples in many regions developed the practice long before refrigeration. The smokehouse lets the catch of a good season last into a lean one."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Parchment is made from animal skin that has been scraped, soaked and stretched. Unlike papyrus, it can be made wherever livestock are raised, which is why it became a writing surface in many different regions. The workshop supplies sheets that stand up to frequent use and travel."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Loose sheets are easily lost; stitched under a cover, they become a book that can be carried and kept. The technique developed wherever paper or parchment was in use, from East Asian bindings to the codex of the Mediterranean. The binder turns writing into a durable object."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A library is more than a store of sheets: it keeps knowledge available to people who did not write it themselves. Collections were gathered by temples, palaces and scholars in many parts of the world. In the town, the library is where written knowledge becomes science."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A saw turns a log into planks, which are easier to carry and build with than the whole trunk. Woodworking is among the oldest crafts, since wood is abundant and easy to shape. The workshop supplies every other trade that needs timber."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A market is a place where goods change hands regularly rather than by chance encounter. Such meeting places exist in every farming society, from the highlands of the Americas to the river towns of Asia and Africa. The town's market turns trade into a lasting institution."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A coin is a piece of metal of a known weight and mark, which saves weighing it at every exchange. Striking coins needs a die, a hammer and a ruler whose mark is trusted. The mint gives the town's trade a measure that travels beyond it."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A tax must be counted, stored and carried somewhere before it can pay for anything. Keeping the dues in one yard makes that work visible and gives the treasury a predictable income. Tax systems appear wherever a city has expenses no single household can meet."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Paper begins as plant fibre beaten into pulp and dried into thin sheets. The craft began in East Asia and reached other regions over centuries of trade; almost any fibre-rich plant can serve as raw material. A paper workshop gives writing a surface cheaper than parchment."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Water-driven stampers beat fibre into pulp far faster than hands can, so a mill can keep several batches in production. The principle is the same as in the water mill and the fulling of cloth, applied to a different material. The mill makes paper a bulk good."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Sun and air preserve food by removing the water that spoilage needs. Drying is among the oldest methods of preservation: fruit, grain, fish and herbs were dried in every climate with a dry season. The dryer's house makes that method available whatever the weather outside."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Tobacco was first used by the peoples of the Americas, long before it reached other continents. The leaf is cured and cut before it is smoked or prepared in other ways. The workshop turns a raw crop into a good the citizens can use."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A scriptorium is a room set aside for copying and correcting texts. Scribes worked in palaces, temples and monasteries in many regions; their careful copies carried much of the ancient world's writing to later generations. Here that work produces science for the town."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Colour can be fixed into cloth with plant matter, minerals and a mordant. Dyeing traditions developed independently wherever textiles were woven, using local plants and minerals. The dyer's house gives the weaver's product its final value."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Metal and stone can be worked into ornaments that carry meaning beyond their weight. Jewellers used whatever their region supplied — gold, silver, copper, shell, jade — so the craft never depended on one material. The house turns valuable raw goods into objects the town can keep or trade."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Before chemistry had a name, workers heated, dissolved and combined substances in search of useful transformations. The tradition drew on metalworking, medicine and dyeing in many regions. The alchemist's house is where such experiments are carried out deliberately."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A barrel is made of staves held by hoops: the shape is strong, stackable and easy to roll. Coopering spread wherever goods had to be carried in bulk, from wine and oil to grain and tar. The cooper's house supplies the containers other workshops cannot do without."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Aromatic resins, flowers and spices can be ground, mixed and burned to give a scent. Perfumes and incense were made in many ancient cultures, from East Asia to the Mediterranean and the Americas. The house works the raw resin into goods worth more than the material itself."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Sugarcane was cultivated in South and Southeast Asia long before sugar reached other regions; beet and other sweet plants later gave the craft a second source. Boiling the juice drives off water so that sugar can be stored and carried. The sugar boiler turns a bulky juice into a compact sweet."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Wood can be joined into a chair, a chest or a table as well as cut into planks. Furniture traditions differ widely — low stools, raised beds, folding screens — but the joinery answers the same needs. The house makes the town's homes usable and, at its best, luxurious."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "Distillation separates a volatile liquid from a fermented mash by boiling it and cooling the vapour. The technique is known in many traditions, from the Near East to the highlands of the Americas, and serves medicine as well as drink. The distillery carries it out on a scale a household cannot."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A candle is fuel given a shape so that it burns slowly: tallow from rendered fat or wax from a hive. Candle-making existed wherever those materials were at hand, and light after dark extended the working day. The chandler supplies mines, workshops and homes."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A sail is a piece of cloth cut and reinforced so that the wind can pull a boat. The craft grew with sailing itself, in every region where boats moved people and goods, and later served the windmill as well. The sailmaker turns woven canvas into power."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A wooden boat needs a frame, planking and a way to seal the seams, all of which a yard can keep in repair. Boatbuilding traditions arose independently on rivers, lakes and coasts across the world. The yard builds the hull; the sailmaker and the ropemaker supply the rest."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "buildings.flavor"
msgid "A university is not a larger scriptorium: the work changes from copying what was written to teaching it and asking what else might be true. Houses of higher learning took different names in different regions — academies, madrasas, monastery schools, guilds of masters — but each kept texts, teachers and pupils together in one place."
msgstr ""

